## Appendix A Combining Run2008 and Run2005 Measurements

### Method for Combining Two Measurements

There are at least two methods combining multiple measurements of the same quantities. The so called "PDG" method is the standard weighing method that is described in the section of "average and fits" in every PDG publication [21]. The other one is the "Best Linear Unbiased Estimate" (BLUE) method [22]. The "BLUE" method is more general and is able to breakdown statistical error from systematic errors and different component of systematic errors as well.

### "PDG " way of Combining two Measurements

#### a.1.1 Two measurements are independent

Two measurements are \(x_{1}\pm\sigma_{1}\) and \(x_{2}\pm\sigma_{2}\), where \(\sigma_{1}\) and \(\sigma_{2}\) are total errors, i.e.

\[\sigma_{i}=\sqrt{(\sigma_{i}^{stat})^{2}+\left(\sigma_{i}^{syst}\right)^{2}}\]

Assuming there is no correlation between \(\sigma_{1}\) and \(\sigma_{2}\), the combined result is \(\langle x\rangle\pm\sigma\)

\[\langle x\rangle=\frac{x_{1}/\sigma_{1}^{2}+x_{1}/\sigma_{2}^{2}}{1/\sigma_{1 }^{2}+1/\sigma_{2}^{2}}\................................\ (1)\]

\[\sigma=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1/\sigma_{1}^{2}+1/\sigma_{2}^{2}}}\................\ (2)\]

