### "BLUE" way of Combining two Measurements

The following are mostly from ref. [22]. The weights \(\omega_{1}\) and \(\omega_{2}\) for calculating combined result, i.e. \(\langle x\rangle=\omega_{1}x_{1}+\omega_{2}x_{2}\), are obtained my minimizing the \(\chi^{2}\) with respect to \(\langle x\rangle\), namely \(\chi^{2}=Q^{t}E^{-1}Q\), where

\[Q=\binom{\langle x\rangle-x_{1}}{\langle x\rangle-x_{2}}\]

, and E is the covariance matrix

\[E=\binom{\sigma_{1}^{2}\quad\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}{\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2 }}\]

\[E^{-1}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{1}^{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}(1-p^{2})}\binom{\sigma_{2}^{2} \quad-\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}{-\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}\]

Therefore,

\[\chi^{2}=\frac{\Delta x_{1}^{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}+\Delta x_{2}^{2}\sigma_{1}^{2} -2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}\Delta x_{1}\Delta x_{2}}{\sigma_{1}^{2}\sigma_{2} ^{2}(1-\rho^{2})}.................. \tag{6}\]

, where \(\Delta x_{1}=\langle x\rangle-x_{1}\) and \(\Delta x_{2}=\langle x\rangle-x_{2}\)

Minimizing \(\chi^{2}\) with respect to \(\langle x\rangle\), one obtain

\[\omega_{1}=\frac{\sigma_{2}(\sigma_{2}-\rho\sigma_{1})}{\sigma_{1}^{2}+\sigma _{2}^{2}-2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}.................. \tag{7}\]

\[\omega_{2}=\frac{\sigma_{1}(\sigma_{1}-\rho\sigma_{2})}{\sigma_{1}^{2}+\sigma _{2}^{2}-2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}.................. \tag{8}\]The combined result is

\[\langle x\rangle=\frac{\sigma_{2}(\sigma_{2}-\rho\sigma_{1})x_{1}+\sigma_{1}( \sigma_{1}-\rho\sigma_{2})x_{2}}{\sigma_{1}^{2}+\sigma_{2}^{2}-2\rho\sigma_{1} \sigma_{2}}.................. \tag{9}\]

The total uncertainty is

\[\sigma=\sqrt{\frac{\sigma_{1}^{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}(1-\rho^{2})}{\sigma_{1}^{2}+ \sigma_{2}^{2}-2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}.................. \tag{10}\]

The combined statistical error is

\[\sigma^{stat}=\sqrt{\omega_{1}^{2}(\sigma_{1}^{stat})^{2}+\omega_{2}^{2}( \sigma_{2}^{stat})^{2}}\]

\[=\frac{\sqrt{(\sigma_{2}(\sigma_{2}-\rho\sigma_{1}))^{2}\big{(}\sigma_{1}^{stat }\big{)}^{2}+(\sigma_{1}(\sigma_{1}-\rho\sigma_{2}))^{2}\big{(}\sigma_{2}^{ stat}\big{)}^{2}}}{\sigma_{1}^{2}+\sigma_{2}^{2}-2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}.................. \tag{11}\]

, where \(\sigma_{i}^{stat}\) is the statistical error of each measurements.

The combined total systematic error is

\[\sigma^{syst}=\sqrt{\sigma^{2}-(\sigma^{stat})^{2}}.................. \tag{12}\]

### How to calculate correlation coefficient (p)

The equation to calculate the correlation coefficient is

\[\rho=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\rho_{i}\sigma_{1,i}^{syst}\sigma_{2,i}^{syst}}{\sigma_{ 1}\sigma_{2}}................................. \tag{13}\]

, where \(\sigma_{1,i}^{syst}\) and \(\sigma_{2,i}^{syst}\) are the breakdown of systematic error for the first and second measurement, respectively. \(\rho_{i}\) is the correlation coefficient for the ith systematic error between the two measurements. To accurately calculate the \(\rho_{i}\)one need to know the P.D.F. of each systematic error. Since this is practically hard to do, we assign either \(\rho_{i}=0\)_or_\(\rho_{i}=\)1 which means.

* p=0 when two measurements are independent,
* p=1 when two measurement are fully correlated
* p= -1 when two measurements are fully anticorrelated.

See chapter A2 for detailed assignment for different category of systematic errors.

### Breakdown the combined systematic errors

The advantage of "BLUE" method is that it allows the breakdown of different types of systematic errors, e.g. point to point and scaling errors. For the ith systematic error

\[\sigma_{i}^{syst}=\sqrt{\left(\omega_{1}\ \omega_{2}\right)\left(\begin{matrix} \left(\sigma_{1,i}^{syst}\right)^{2}&\rho_{i}\sigma_{1,i}^{syst}\sigma_{2,i} ^{syst}\\ \rho_{i}\sigma_{1,i}^{syst}\sigma_{2,i}^{syst}&\left(\sigma_{2,i}^{syst} \right)^{2}\end{matrix}\right)\left(\begin{matrix}\omega_{1}\\ \omega_{2}\end{matrix}\right)}\]\[=\sqrt{\left(\omega_{1}\sigma_{1,i}^{syst}\right)^{2}+\left(\omega_{2}\sigma_{2,i}^ {syst}\right)^{2}+2\omega_{1}\omega_{2}\rho_{i}\sigma_{1,i}^{syst}\sigma_{2,i}^ {syst}},\................. \tag{14}\]

where \(\sigma_{i}^{syst}\) is the \(i\)th systematic error, \(\sigma_{1,i}^{syst}\) is the \(i\)th systematic error of the first measurements and \(\sigma_{2,i}^{syst}\) is the \(i\)th systematic error of the second measurement.

### \(\chi^{2}\) test to quantify the consistency between two measurement

The \(\chi^{2}\) obtained from eq.(6) are for two measurements of the same observable. To compare if two \(\rm p_{T}\) spectra are consistent with each other, one can treat different \(\rm p_{T}\) bin as different observable. Assuming there is no correlation among different \(\rm p_{T}\) bins, the total \(\chi^{2}\) can be calculated as the following

\[\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\chi_{i}^{2}................. \tag{15}\]

, where N is the number of \(\rm p_{T}\) bins, \(\chi_{i}^{2}\) is the number for each \(\rm p_{T}\) bin as calculated from eq. (6). If all individual error are Gaussian, the value calculate from eq. (15) should follow \(\chi^{2}\) distribution of N degree of freedom.

### Relation between "PDG" and "BLUE" method

When two measurements are totally independent, \(\rm\rho\)=0, then eq.(7) and (8) become eq.(1), (2). In the case that two measurement are correlated, if we take \(\Delta^{2}\)= \(\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}\), then equation (7) and (8) become eq.(4) and (5).

